---
import "../styles/global.css";
import BallotPaper from "../components/BallotPaper.astro";
import CandidateStack from "../components/CandidateStack.astro";
import VoteSlider from "../components/VoteSlider.astro";
import { scenarioExplore } from "../data/scenario-explore";
import { scenarioSpoiler } from "../data/scenario-spoiler";
import { strategicBallot } from "../data/sample-ballot-strategic";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-AU">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Who Really Won? — FPTP vs. preferential voting</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header>
      <nav aria-label="Primary">
        <a href="./">Who Really Won?</a>
      </nav>
    </header>
    <main>
      <h1>Who Really Won?</h1>
      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="fptp-ballot-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Every voter gets one ballot paper. Under first-past-the-post, you
            tick just one box — your favourite candidate — and that's the
            only preference this system ever sees.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz chapter-viz-intro" id="fptp-ballot-intro">
          <h2 id="fptp-ballot-heading">How the ballot works</h2>
          <BallotPaper
            candidates={scenarioExplore.candidates}
            ranking={scenarioExplore.groups[0].ranking}
            variant="full"
            mode="fptp"
            hero
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="explore-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Every voter ranks the candidates in order, but under
            first-past-the-post only a voter's first preference is ever
            counted. Try it yourself — move the sliders and watch who's
            ahead.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="explore-app">
          <h2 id="explore-heading">How first-past-the-post works</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioExplore.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                  <VoteSlider id={candidate.id} label={candidate.label} />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="spoiler-chapter" aria-labelledby="spoiler-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Now here's one particular election. Aster and Birch are similar
            candidates who split a majority that would rather see either of
            them win over Cedar — so under first-past-the-post, Cedar takes
            it on a minority of first preferences. Try the sliders: as long
            as Aster and Birch are close together, Cedar keeps winning even
            though most voters prefer someone else.
          </p>
          <BallotPaper
            candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
            ranking={scenarioSpoiler.groups[0].ranking}
            variant="full"
            mode="fptp"
            hero
          />
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="spoiler-app">
          <h2 id="spoiler-heading">Where first-past-the-post goes wrong</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioSpoiler.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                  <VoteSlider id={candidate.id} label={candidate.label} />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="strategic-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Under first-past-the-post, a vote for a candidate who can't win
            is a vote that changes nothing. Take one Birch supporter from the
            scenario above: Birch is the weaker of the two similar
            candidates, so backing them sincerely leaves Cedar's plurality
            untouched. Backing Aster instead — their genuine second choice —
            actually changes the outcome.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz">
          <h2 id="strategic-heading">Why voters vote tactically</h2>
          <div class="ballot-comparison">
            <div>
              <h3>Sincere vote</h3>
              <BallotPaper
                candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
                ranking={strategicBallot.sincereRanking}
                variant="full"
                mode="fptp"
              />
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Tactical vote</h3>
              <BallotPaper
                candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
                ranking={strategicBallot.tacticalRanking}
                variant="full"
                mode="fptp"
              />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="duverger-heading">
        <h2 id="duverger-heading">Why elections converge to two parties</h2>
        <p>
          This is the pattern behind Duverger's law: when voters know that
          backing their sincere favourite can hand the win to their least
          preferred candidate, they drift toward whichever of the two
          strongest contenders they dislike least. Over enough elections,
          first-past-the-post tends to squeeze out third parties and settle
          into a two-party contest — not because voters stop having a wider
          range of preferences, but because the system only ever listens to
          the first one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="irv-ballot-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            This time your ballot asks for more: number every candidate in
            the order you actually prefer them — 1 for your favourite, 2 for
            your next, and so on, all the way down.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz chapter-viz-intro" id="irv-ballot-intro">
          <h2 id="irv-ballot-heading">How preferential ballots work</h2>
          <BallotPaper
            candidates={scenarioSpoiler.candidates}
            ranking={scenarioSpoiler.groups[0].ranking}
            variant="full"
            mode="irv"
            hero
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="recount-heading">
        <div class="chapter-prose">
          <p>
            Here's that same election again, but counted a different way.
            Instant-runoff voting uses every voter's full ranking: if no one
            has a majority of first preferences, the last-place candidate is
            eliminated and their ballots move to each voter's next choice.
            Step through the recount and watch who wins this time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter-viz" id="recount-app">
          <h2 id="recount-heading">Recounting the same election under IRV</h2>
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-ballot-drift>
            {
              scenarioSpoiler.candidates.map((candidate) => (
                <div class="candidate-column">
                  <CandidateStack
                    id={candidate.id}
                    label={candidate.label}
                    colour={candidate.colour}
                    shape={candidate.shape}
                  />
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p data-testid="round-status" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <div class="recount-controls">
            <button type="button" data-action="prev-round">Previous round</button>
            <button type="button" data-action="next-round">Next round</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="freeplay-heading">
        <h2 id="freeplay-heading">Try it yourself, no script this time</h2>
        <p>
          Every example above was tuned to make a point. This one isn't — add
          or remove candidates, drag the sliders however you like, and see
          who first-past-the-post picks.
        </p>
        <div id="freeplay-app">
          <div class="candidate-columns" data-freeplay-columns></div>
          <p data-testid="winner" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <div class="freeplay-controls">
            <button type="button" data-action="add-candidate">
              Add candidate
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <script>
      import "../scripts/bootstrap";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
